From: James Daugherty <jhdaugh@garnet.msen.com>
Date: Wed, 1 Jun 1994 05:07:02 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-6-2] SCI: Chaos, Complexity and Reductionism

On Sun, 8 May 1994, Marvin Minsky wrote:

> I tend to agreee strongly with Mike Price.   By feeling is that when A says
> that "P is an emergent phenomenon, A means that "I do not see how P is a
> predictable possible consequence of the elements, interactions, and initial
> conditions of system S".  In other words "emergence" is a sense of surprise
> in an observer, not a mysterious propert of a system.  Science -- that is,
> reductionism -- proceeds by examining things until one understand enough to
> be no longer surprised.
> 
> >Of course, I am not denying that molecules and subatomic particles
> >exist or that emergent entities are in no way impacted by their components.
> >Perhaps we mean different things by reductionism?
> 
> Can you say briefly what you mean by reductionism?
> 
> >Have you read and rejected Rupert Sheldrake?
> 
> I haven't.  Is it possible to state briefly what you consider a very good
> idea of his?  I don't usually read things until someone tells me, not that
> "Xis good to read" but "Here is a very good idea found in X".
> >James H. Daugherty,  jhdaugh@garnet.msen.com
> 

To restart the above thread I am quoting fro Matter and Consciousness by 
Paul Churchland to attempt to show what is wrong with materialist 
reductionist thought which I believe was echoed by my critics:

Page 12:

"But any property dualist makes the further claim that mental states and 
properties are irreducible, in the sense that they are not just 
organizational features of physical matter, as are the examples cited 
(solid, colored, alive).  They are said to be properties beyond 
prediction or explanation of physical science."

The above is clearly a straw man argument.

Some religionists may say such things, but certainly not everyone that 
talks about emergent properties.

I for one think that if one knew the necessary organization of matter and 
could reproduce it, the same or similar (depending upon varying initial 
conditions) emergent property would be reproduced.

The point of emergent properties is that a new entity with capacities in 
some ways independent of the constituent parts comes into being.

In other words, the actions of the emergent entity would not be entirely 
due to the cause and effect chains or random statistical actions of its 
components.

Re:  Sheldrake's Presence of the Past:  Morphic Resonance and the Habits 
of Nature.  I cannot currently summarize its important parts as I loaned 
out my hardcover and the paperback just went out-of-print!

I will post when I find a copy.

EOM


