From: "R. David Murray" <rdmurray@keene.edu>
Date: Fri, 3 Jun 1994 21:26:52 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-6-6] BOOK: Fuzzy Thinking

Just finished reading "Fuzzy Thinking" (Bart Kosko) that I picked up at 
Extro I.  While I found the book itself a little repetetive (he keeps 
hammering at themes and motifs over and over; good pedagogy, perhaps, but 
I got a little tired of it by the end), I also found the ideas very 
compelling.  (And despite my caveat above the book is quite readable and 
in fact engaging!)

The ideas had some of the same "a ha" reaction that I had to Machinery of
Freedom, and that Max said he had about PCR.  Fuzzy thinking provides an
interesting perspective on supposedly black and white questions like
abortion (discussed in the book). 

It also provides a *very* interesting perspective on the upload question: 
the you that exists now is to a large extent the you that existed ten 
minutes ago, but it is also to some extent not you.  The you that got up 
this morning is to some large extent the you that went to bed last night, 
but also to some extent not you.  The you that gets uploaded is to a 
large extent the biological you, but to some extent it is not you.  This 
is the way of the world.  So it is not a matter of choosing gradual 
upload so that it is certain to be you vs catastrophic upload which might 
be you to only 95%.  What you get to choose is how much youness is 
required before you will upload.  Choose your fuzz threshold.

I also found it amusing that from beginning to end Kosko is arguing that 
the true nature of the universe is (A and Not A) as opposed to the 
Aristotelian (A or Not A).  So Tom Morrow's goal of boundlessly expanding 
beyond the rule of (A or Not A) is already accomplished . . . .

--David

